1. AOSP Architecture Overview
Android follows a layered architecture:
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    Applications Layer                        │
│          (System Apps, Third-party Apps, OEM Apps)          │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                  Application Framework                       │
│    (Activity Manager, Window Manager, Content Providers,    │
│     Package Manager, Telephony Manager, Resource Manager)   │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                    Native Libraries                          │
│  (libc, liblog, libutils, Surface Manager, Media Framework) │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                Android Runtime (ART)                         │
│              (Core Libraries, Dalvik VM)                     │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│            Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL)                  │
│   (Camera HAL, Sensors HAL, Audio HAL, Graphics HAL, etc.)  │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                     Linux Kernel                             │
│   (Drivers, Power Management, Memory Management, Binder)    │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

Key Points:
* Each layer provides services to the layer above
* HAL provides a standard interface between Android framework and hardware
* Binder IPC enables communication between processes

