├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                  │
│   Camera HAL Process                                            │
│        │                                                         │
│        ├── Can read /data/user/* (user's private data) ❌       │
│        ├── Can write to /system/* (modify system files) ❌      │
│        ├── Can access network (send data anywhere) ❌           │
│        └── Can access any device node ❌                        │
│                                                                  │
│   If hacked, attacker has FULL access to everything!           │
│                                                                  │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    WITH SELinux (Safe)                          │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                  │
│   Camera HAL Process (restricted to camera_hal domain)         │
│        │                                                         │
│        ├── Can ONLY read camera device nodes ✅                 │
│        ├── Can ONLY write to camera data directory ✅           │
│        ├── CANNOT access user data 🚫                           │
│        ├── CANNOT modify system files 🚫                        │
│        └── CANNOT access network 🚫                             │
│                                                                  │
│   If hacked, attacker is CONTAINED to camera only!             │
│                                                                  │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

SELinux Key Concepts
Concept	What It Is	Example
Domain	Security label for a PROCESS	example_camera_hal
Type	Security label for a FILE/RESOURCE	camera_device
Policy	Rules that say "domain X can do Y to type Z"	allow camera_hal camera_device:chr_file read;
Context	Full security label	u:r:example_camera_hal:s0
📚 Part 2: The Two Main Files We Create
Overview of SELinux Files
sepolicy/
├── example_camera_hal.te    ← POLICY FILE (Type Enforcement)
│                               Defines: domain, rules, permissions
│
└── file_contexts            ← LABELING FILE
                               Defines: which files get which labels


📝 Part 3: Creating the Type Enforcement File (.te)
Step 1: Define the Domain (Process Label)
# File: sepolicy/example_camera_hal.te

# ============================================================================
# STEP 1: Define types (labels)
# ============================================================================

# This creates a NEW domain called "example_camera_hal"
# Think of it as creating a new "identity" for our process
type example_camera_hal, domain;
#    ↑                   ↑
#    Name of our         "domain" is a special attribute
#    security domain     that marks this as a process type

What this means:
* Every process in Android runs in a "domain"
* Our camera HAL will run in the example_camera_hal domain
* Any rules we write will apply to processes in this domain
Step 2: Define the Executable Type
# This creates a label for our binary file
type example_camera_hal_exec, exec_type, vendor_file_type, file_type;
#    ↑                        ↑          ↑                 ↑
#    Label name               Marks as   Marks as          Generic
#                             executable vendor file       file type

What this means:
* Our binary android.hardware.camera.provider@2.7-service.meta
* Will be labeled as example_camera_hal_exec
* When init starts this binary, it will transition to example_camera_hal domain
Step 3: Define Data Directory Type
# Label for our data files
type example_camera_hal_data_file, file_type, data_file_type;
#    ↑                             ↑          ↑
#    Label name                    Generic    Data partition
#                                  file       file type

What this means:
* Files in camera  will be labeled example_camera_hal_data_file
* Our HAL can read/write files with this label
* Other processes CANNOT access these files (unless we allow them)
Step 4: Enable Domain Transition
# ============================================================================
# STEP 2: Domain transition (how process gets our domain)
# ============================================================================

# This macro sets up the transition:
# When init executes example_camera_hal_exec → process enters example_camera_hal domain
init_daemon_domain(example_camera_hal)

Visual explanation of domain transition:
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    Domain Transition Flow                        │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                  │
│   1. Init process (domain: init)                                │
│      reads init.rc file                                         │
│            │                                                     │
│            ▼                                                     │
│   2. Init finds: "service camera.provider ... /vendor/bin/hw/..." │
│            │                                                     │
│            ▼                                                     │
│   3. Init executes the binary                                   │
│      Binary has label: example_camera_hal_exec                  │
│            │                                                     │
│            ▼ ← TRANSITION HAPPENS HERE (because of macro)       │
│   4. New process runs in domain: example_camera_hal             │
│                                                                  │
│   The macro "init_daemon_domain" makes step 3→4 happen!        │
│                                                                  │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

Step 5: Add Binder Permissions
# ============================================================================
# STEP 3: Binder IPC permissions (for HIDL/AIDL communication)
# ============================================================================

# Allow our HAL to use Binder (Android's IPC mechanism)
binder_use(example_camera_hal)

# Allow two-way communication with cameraserver
binder_call(example_camera_hal, cameraserver)
binder_call(cameraserver, example_camera_hal)

What these rules mean:
┌──────────────────┐         Binder IPC         ┌──────────────────┐
│  Camera Server   │ ◄─────────────────────────► │  Camera HAL      │
│  (Framework)     │                             │  (Our HAL)       │
│                  │    binder_call allows       │                  │
│  domain:         │    bidirectional            │  domain:         │
│  cameraserver    │    communication            │  example_camera  │
│                  │                             │  _hal            │
└──────────────────┘                             └──────────────────┘

Step 6: Register as HAL Service












# Allow registration with Hardware Service Manager
hal_server_domain(example_camera_hal, hal_camera_provider)
#               ↑                    ↑
#               Our domain           HAL type we implement

# Allow finding/adding HIDL services
allow example_camera_hal hwservicemanager:hwservice_manager { add find };

What this does:
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                  Service Registration Flow                       │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                  │
│   Our HAL Process                                               │
│        │                                                         │
│        │ registerAsService("meta/example/0")                    │
│        ▼                                                         │
│   ┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐                   │
│   │       Hardware Service Manager           │                   │
│   │                                          │                   │
│   │  Services Table:                         │                   │
│   │  ┌──────────────────────────────────┐   │                   │
│   │  │ meta/example/0 → our_binder_ref  │   │ ← "add" permission│
│   │  └──────────────────────────────────┘   │                   │
│   └─────────────────────────────────────────┘                   │
│        │                                                         │
│        │ Camera Framework calls getService("meta/example/0")    │
│        ▼                                                         │
│   Framework finds our HAL ← "find" permission needed            │
│                                                                  │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

Step 7: Hardware Access Permissions
# ============================================================================
# STEP 4: Hardware access (camera sensors, ISP, etc.)
# ============================================================================

# Access to camera device nodes (/dev/video*, /dev/camera*)
allow example_camera_hal camera_device:chr_file { read write open ioctl getattr };
#     ↑                   ↑            ↑         ↑
#     Our domain          Target type  Target    Operations
#     (process)           (file)       class     allowed

Breaking down the rule:
allow example_camera_hal camera_device:chr_file { read write open ioctl getattr };
       ↓                       ↓           ↓              ↓
     SOURCE                  TARGET      CLASS         PERMISSIONS
   (who wants               (what        (what         (what can
    to access)              type)        kind)         be done)

Translates to English:
"Allow processes in example_camera_hal domain
 to read, write, open, ioctl, and getattr
 on character device files
 that have the camera_device type label"

Step 8: File System Permissions
# ============================================================================
# STEP 5: File system access
# ============================================================================

# Our data directory (/data/vendor/camera/)
allow example_camera_hal example_camera_hal_data_file:file {
    create read write open unlink getattr setattr
};
allow example_camera_hal example_camera_hal_data_file:dir {
    create read write open search add_name remove_name
};

Visual of what this allows:
/data/vendor/camera/                 ← dir operations
├── config.xml                       ← file operations
├── calibration.dat                  ← file operations
└── cache/                           ← dir operations
    └── frame_001.raw               ← file operations

All these have label: example_camera_hal_data_file

Operations allowed:
├── create  - Create new files/directories
├── read    - Read content
├── write   - Modify content
├── open    - Open file handles
├── search  - Search in directories
├── add_name/remove_name - Add/remove directory entries
└── getattr/setattr - Read/modify file attributes

Step 9: Neverallow Rules (Security Boundaries)
# ============================================================================
# STEP 6: Security boundaries (what we CANNOT do)
# ============================================================================

# Our HAL cannot access user app data
neverallow example_camera_hal app_data_file:file *;
#          ↑                   ↑              ↑
#          Our domain          App data       ALL operations
#                              type           denied

# Our HAL cannot modify system files
neverallow example_camera_hal system_file:file execute;

Why neverallow matters:
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    Neverallow Rules                              │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                  │
│   If we write:                                                  │
│   neverallow example_camera_hal app_data_file:file *;           │
│                                                                  │
│   And later someone adds:                                       │
│   allow example_camera_hal app_data_file:file read;             │
│                                                                  │
│   ❌ BUILD FAILS! SELinux compiler catches the conflict        │
│                                                                  │
│   This prevents accidental security holes!                      │
│                                                                  │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘


📝 Part 4: Creating the File Contexts
What is file_contexts?
The file_contexts file tells SELinux which security label to apply to which files.
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    file_contexts Purpose                         │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                  │
│   When Android boots, it applies labels to files:               │
│                                                                  │
│   /vendor/bin/hw/camera.provider@2.7-service.meta               │
│        ↓                                                         │
│   file_contexts says: "label this as example_camera_hal_exec"   │
│        ↓                                                         │
│   File gets context: u:object_r:example_camera_hal_exec:s0      │
│                                                                  │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

File Contexts Syntax
# Format:
# <path_regex>  <file_type>  <security_context>

/vendor/bin/hw/android\.hardware\.camera\.provider@2\.7-service\.meta  u:object_r:example_camera_hal_exec:s0
#  ↑                                                                     ↑
#  Path pattern (regex)                                                  Security context

Breaking Down the Security Context
u:object_r:example_camera_hal_exec:s0
↑    ↑           ↑                  ↑
│    │           │                  └── MLS Level (Multi-Level Security)
│    │           │                      s0 = lowest level (default)
│    │           │
│    │           └── TYPE
│    │               This is what SELinux uses for access decisions!
│    │               Must match a type defined in .te file
│    │
│    └── ROLE
│        object_r = for files/objects (not processes)
│        r = for processes (like u:r:example_camera_hal:s0)
│
└── USER
    u = standard user (always "u" in Android)

Step-by-Step: Creating file_contexts
# File: sepolicy/file_contexts

# ============================================================================
# EXECUTABLE FILES
# ============================================================================

# Our camera HAL service binary
# The . is escaped as \. because it's regex
/vendor/bin/hw/android\.hardware\.camera\.provider@2\.7-service\.meta  u:object_r:example_camera_hal_exec:s0

Why escape the dots?
Pattern:  /vendor/bin/hw/android.hardware.camera...
          In regex, . means "any character"
          
So:       /vendor/bin/hw/androidXhardwareXcamera...
          Would also match! (X = any char)

Fixed:    /vendor/bin/hw/android\.hardware\.camera...
          Now . only matches literal dots

Labeling Configuration Files
# ============================================================================
# CONFIGURATION FILES
# ============================================================================

# Camera configuration directory and all files within
/vendor/etc/camera(/.*)?  u:object_r:vendor_configs_file:s0
#                  ↑  ↑
#                  │  └── ? = 0 or 1 occurrence
#                  └── (/.*)  = optional / followed by anything

# This matches:
# /vendor/etc/camera              ← directory itself
# /vendor/etc/camera/config.xml   ← files inside
# /vendor/etc/camera/sub/file.dat ← nested files

Labeling Data Directories
# ============================================================================
# DATA DIRECTORIES
# ============================================================================

# Camera persistent data
/data/vendor/camera(/.*)?  u:object_r:example_camera_hal_data_file:s0

# This matches:
# /data/vendor/camera/
# /data/vendor/camera/calibration.dat
# /data/vendor/camera/cache/frame.raw

Labeling Device Nodes
# ============================================================================
# DEVICE NODES
# ============================================================================

# Video capture devices (camera sensors)
/dev/video[0-9]+  u:object_r:video_device:s0
#          ↑
#          Matches video0, video1, ... video9, video10, etc.

# Camera-specific devices
/dev/camera[0-9]+  u:object_r:camera_device:s0

# ISP (Image Signal Processor) devices
/dev/isp[0-9]+  u:object_r:isp_device:s0

Complete file_contexts Example
# ============================================================================
# Complete file_contexts for Camera HAL
# ============================================================================

# === EXECUTABLE ===
/vendor/bin/hw/android\.hardware\.camera\.provider@2\.7-service\.meta  u:object_r:example_camera_hal_exec:s0

# === CONFIGURATION ===
/vendor/etc/camera(/.*)?  u:object_r:vendor_configs_file:s0
/vendor/etc/camera/calibration(/.*)?  u:object_r:vendor_configs_file:s0

# === DATA ===
/data/vendor/camera(/.*)?  u:object_r:example_camera_hal_data_file:s0

# === DEVICES ===
/dev/video[0-9]+  u:object_r:video_device:s0
/dev/camera[0-9]+  u:object_r:camera_device:s0
/dev/isp[0-9]+  u:object_r:isp_device:s0

# === SYSFS ===
/sys/devices/platform/camera(/.*)?  u:object_r:sysfs_camera:s0
/sys/class/camera(/.*)?  u:object_r:sysfs_camera:s0


